Tokenism Fatigue in Professional Settings - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Other problem related to employment)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Sole representative depletion, Diversity marketing frustration, Conformity suppression

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-047
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of intractable tension headaches, severe gastrointestinal distress, and profound physical exhaustion mimicking an acute viral or metabolic illness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe exacerbation of unremitting headache, nausea, and a complete inability to stand without dizziness following a major corporate event. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to definitively rule out a severe migraine, acute gastroenteritis, or severe dehydration prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My head is splitting, my stomach is in knots, and I am so exhausted from smiling for their brochures while they ignore everything I actually say.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a progressive history of profound emotional depletion from being the sole representative of a marginalized group at their workplace. Over the past 72 hours, following a major corporate diversity initiative where the patient was heavily utilized for marketing, they experienced severe diversity marketing frustration and profound tokenism fatigue. This triggered an acute physical collapse characterized by intractable tension headaches, chronic gastrointestinal distress (nausea, cramping), and severe burnout from the dual burden of standard job duties and unofficial DEI representation. The patient reports chronic imposter syndrome exacerbated by the suspicion of being a diversity hire, intense pressure to consistently outperform peers, and deep resentment toward leadership. They note a chronic, exhausting suppression of authentic cultural expression to conform to dominant workplace norms.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; bedbound due to severe headache, nausea, and physical exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of profound occupational burnout and long-term disability due to unmanaged, systemic workplace exploitation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, deeply fatigued, holding their head, and moving sluggishly.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild reactive tachycardia, normal heart sounds. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Abdomen: Soft, non-distended. Diffuse, mild tenderness on deep palpation without guarding or rebound, consistent with stress-induced visceral hypersensitivity. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. No focal deficits. Musculoskeletal: Severe, bilateral spasm of the temporalis, masseter, and occipital muscles. The clinical interview highlights sole representative depletion; the patient's affect is profoundly blunted, and they struggle to recount the timeline of the work event due to cognitive fatigue. Conformity suppression is evident as the patient expresses intense relief at not having to 'code-switch' or perform for the visiting clinician.
Environmental Observations: A corporate award or plaque is visible on a desk, pushed face down.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. Has taken over-the-counter ibuprofen with no relief for the headache.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Tokenism Fatigue in Professional Settings - Adjustment Disorder - V62.29 (Other problem related to employment) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of significant diarrhea, fever, and the clear correlation of the nausea specifically with the intense psychosocial stress of the corporate event, rather than an infectious exposure.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, negative nuchal rigidity (neck is supple despite muscle tension), and no photophobia/phonophobia out of proportion to a standard tension headache.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely as the abdominal pain is diffuse, cramping, and lacks the severe, localized, burning epigastric nature typical of an acute ulcer.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, functionally incapacitating somatic symptoms (severe tension headache, nausea, tachycardia) directly resulting from profound occupational overexertion and the psychological trauma of tokenism. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was necessary to perform a physical examination to rule out an acute organic cause for the headache and abdominal pain (e.g., meningitis or surgical abdomen), and to provide immediate medical certification to halt the unsustainable work cycle. Delaying care until routine hours would have forced the patient to attempt to work through a severe physical crash, risking further physiological breakdown, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral metoclopramide 10mg to manage the nausea and oral naproxen 500mg for the tension headache. Issued a strict 5-day medical certificate for immediate stress leave. Advised the usual GP to review high scores on the Maslach Burnout Inventory and document qualitative reports of extreme workplace isolation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the immediate physical burnout, enforce a "sensory and social blackout": remain in the home for 48 hours with all work communications blocked; completely removing the demand for conformity suppression allows the nervous system to baseline. To mitigate diversity marketing frustration upon return to work, draft a "scope of practice boundary": write a polite but firm email template declining all future marketing requests that fall outside the official job description; having the template ready removes the anxiety of having to invent a boundary in the moment.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to an occupational psychologist specializing in minority stress and workplace equity.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 5 days to assess physical recovery, review the medical leave status, and plan a sustainable return-to-work strategy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if the headache becomes sudden and explosive ("thunderclap"), if experiencing uncontrollable vomiting preventing fluid intake, or if developing a high fever.

